Connect material response to geometry history and intended function (@engineering-material-meaning: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "../mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/materials/Stress-strain-measures-and-the-tensile-test.lingua#r_61ZDKD8MXWTDZ7Q1M9SECY1Z5P") { r_0002F52C0C0519A796BF8AB448
A dimensionally correct part can still deform too much in use. Begin with the intended function, the applied interactions, the reference configuration and the change that matters. Reuse [Connect realization to verification and intended use](Connect-realization-to-verification-and-intended-use.lingua#r_0C593F4A7A204FABB3B5876653) and [Investigate an assembly from views to dimensional evidence](Investigate-an-assembly-from-views-to-dimensional-evidence.lingua#r_0389B90567C4C9183DE77AC20A). A requirement about the spacing between two faces is different from a limit on the movement of an actuator or support. Name the locations and conditions before assigning a displacement limit.

Material response and component response are connected but distinct. Modulus describes a specified relation between stress and strain within a material model; the stiffness of a component also depends on its geometry, loading and constraints. Strength concerns a defined failure or unacceptable change. A large modulus does not establish large strength, fatigue resistance, dimensional stability or a useful interface. A tabulated material name alone does not specify composition, processing history, direction, temperature or the conditions under which a reported property was measured.

Draw a straight specimen, its two reference marks, the grips and the surrounding load frame. Applied force is transmitted through every series element. The distance between specimen marks can change less than the total actuator movement because grips and supports also deform. A force–movement slope is therefore evidence about an identified arrangement. It becomes a material estimate only through additional geometry, boundary and measurement assumptions.

Our common model will use straight axial members with positive constant modulus, area and reference length, small deformation, no transverse displacement, quasi-static equilibrium and fixed connections. It permits tensile and compressive axial force algebraically. It does not check lateral buckling, joints, local stress concentrations, plasticity, creep or damage. Those omissions delimit the question; they do not establish that a physical assembly can ignore those mechanisms.

**Reading and work:** [Modules in Mechanics of Materials — David Roylance](../mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/sources/Mechanics-of-Materials-Roylance.lingua#r_7K2RYA9504FGFGJTVWHMWXD5WY), the opening elasticity and stress–strain modules, followed progressively by [Applied Mechanics of Solids — Allan F. Bower](../mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/sources/Applied-Mechanics-of-Solids-Bower.lingua#r_109WFZBWMEMGHV9TGFD22H2F1W) section 3.2's assumptions. Explain one change in geometry that changes component stiffness without changing the material. Write separate requirements for displacement, continued function and evidence about material state. Explain which can be addressed by the coming calculation and which need further observations or models. No structural installation or performed material test is claimed.

**Continue:** [Stress–strain measures and the tensile test](../mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/materials/Stress-strain-measures-and-the-tensile-test.lingua#r_61ZDKD8MXWTDZ7Q1M9SECY1Z5P).

